.TH ZZ9KSVC 7 "1 November 2026" "zz9ksvc"
.SH NAME
zz9ksvc \- the service channel of a ZZ9000-family board's second ARM core
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B /dev/zz9ksvc0
.br
.B /dev/zz9ksvc1
.br
.B #include <sys/zz9ksvc.h>
.SH DESCRIPTION
A Z3660, and a ZZ9000 with a firmware that has the services runtime, can do work
on the board's second ARM core for a program on the Amiga: decompression first,
more services later.
The program hands it buffers in the board's memory and requests, and waits for
the replies.
Each board is a unit, and
.BI /dev/zz9ksvc N
is the channel to unit
.IR N .
.PP
When the board, its bootstrap block or its runtime is missing, the open fails
with
.BR ENXIO :
the program then does the work itself.
.PP
Every open is a client of its own, and the kernel stands between the clients and
the board:
.IP \(bu 3
a client names its buffers by handles, 0 to 15, never by an address;
.IP \(bu
the kernel builds each request it passes on: it gives the request its number,
lets through only the requests it knows, and puts the board's own handle in
place of each handle the client names, after checking the buffer is that
client's and the bytes used lie inside it;
.IP \(bu
no address from a client reaches the board, and no board address reaches a
client.
.PP
Each client may hold 16 buffers and 8 MiB of buffers in all, and have 4
requests in flight.
.PP
The driver is the loadable module
.BR zz9ksvc ,
of the demand class: it loads at the first open, and stays only while a board
with a runtime is there.
The board driver (the Z3660's
.BR z3660rtg ,
a ZZ9000's) finds the board and gives it to
.B zz9ksvc
through the small module
.BR zz9kbus .
.SH IOCTLS
.TP
.B ZZSIOC_INFO
fills a
.BR "struct zzs_info" :
the unit, the board's id, the runtime's ABI version and capability bits, the
ring sizes, this client's limits and the bytes of buffers it holds.
.TP
.B ZZSIOC_ALLOC
allocates a buffer of
.B za_length
bytes, rounded up to whole pages, and gives its handle and, unless
.B ZZS_ALLOC_CARDONLY
is asked, the cookie that maps it.
.B ENOSPC
when the client has no handle or quota left.
.TP
.B ZZSIOC_FREE
frees the buffer whose handle is the argument.
.B EBUSY
while a request in flight uses it.
.TP
.B ZZSIOC_SUBMIT
passes on a request: the opcode and up to 48 bytes of payload in the runtime's
own layout, with each buffer named by its handle.
It gives the request's number.
.B EINVAL
for a request the kernel does not pass on or a range outside its buffer,
.B EBADF
for a handle that is not one of this client's buffers,
.B EPERM
for a request only root may make,
.B EAGAIN
when 4 are in flight or the rings are full.
.TP
.B ZZSIOC_WAIT
waits for the request
.BR zw_id ,
at most
.B zw_ms
milliseconds (10 seconds when 0), and gives the runtime's status and reply.
A signal ends the wait with
.BR EINTR ,
and the request can be waited for again.
.B ETIMEDOUT
when the time runs out (see
.BR "Timeouts" ).
.TP
.B ZZSIOC_STATS
(root) fills a
.B "struct zzs_stats"
with the channel's counts.
.TP
.B ZZSIOC_RESET
(root) forgets everything the runtime held, after it was restarted: requests in
flight fail with
.BR EIO ,
and buffers must be freed and allocated again.
.SS Mapping a buffer
.BR mmap (2)
with
.BR MAP_SHARED ,
the buffer's cookie as the offset (plus a page-aligned offset into the buffer)
and a length within it maps that buffer of the board's memory.
Nothing else on the board can be mapped.
A cookie stops working when its buffer is freed.
.SS Timeouts
The runtime cannot cancel work it has taken.
When a wait times out, the buffers the request uses are quarantined: the runtime
may still write them, so they are not freed or handed out again until its reply
comes or the runtime is reset.
The same happens to a client's buffers when it closes, or is killed, with
requests in flight.
At most 32 buffers are quarantined at a time; a request that could take the
count past that gives
.BR EAGAIN .
.PP
The kernel looks for replies at every clock tick while requests are in flight,
and not at all otherwise.
.SH FILES
.TP
.BR /dev/zz9ksvc0 ", " /dev/zz9ksvc1
character major 55, minors 0 and 1, mode 0666.
.TP
.B /etc/conf/mod.d/zz9ksvc
the module;
.B /etc/conf/mod.d/zz9kbus
the board registry it uses.
.SH NOTES
The runtime's requests and replies are those of the ZZ9000 SDK version 2
(BlitterStudio zz9000-firmware); the module carries its headers.
The module is GPL-3.0-or-later; the interface between board drivers and it,
.BR zz9kbus ,
is MIT.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mmap (2)
